1319
01:01:44,568 --> 01:01:45,860
we're quite interested in this.

1320
01:01:45,860 --> 01:01:48,800
I think we'll be looking at it and hopefully,

1321
01:01:48,800 --> 01:01:50,480
if I can convince Hayden, he'll be

1322
01:01:50,480 --> 01:01:54,640
staring at the activations-- at the actual weights

1323
01:01:54,640 --> 01:01:58,450
to figure out why when you loop it, it gets better.

1324
01:01:58,450 --> 01:01:59,950
Here's just a follow up on that.

1325
01:01:59,950 --> 01:02:03,670
So you talked about compute optimality for the loop models,

1326
01:02:03,670 --> 01:02:06,050
can you speak about the inference implications

1327
01:02:06,050 --> 01:02:09,590
and the memory and how that can make you go faster?

1328
01:02:09,590 --> 01:02:10,090
Yeah.

1329
01:02:10,090 --> 01:02:11,882
So one of the reasons that I was personally

1330
01:02:11,882 --> 01:02:14,010
very excited in these loop things

1331
01:02:14,010 --> 01:02:16,702
is that, one of the big bottlenecks

1332
01:02:16,702 --> 01:02:18,410
to serving inference efficiently actually

1333
01:02:18,410 --> 01:02:20,010
ends up being GPU memory.

1334
01:02:20,010 --> 01:02:22,050
So if you have fewer parameters, you

1335
01:02:22,050 --> 01:02:24,930
can fit, for example, more KV Cache,

1336
01:02:24,930 --> 01:02:27,930
or you can do less communication because you

1337
01:02:27,930 --> 01:02:31,390
need to split your model against fewer GPUs and things like that.

1338
01:02:31,390 --> 01:02:33,730
So there's actually a lot of flexibility

1339
01:02:33,730 --> 01:02:36,810
that a smaller model will get you.

1340
01:02:36,810 --> 01:02:38,370
I also had this dream that if you

1341
01:02:38,370 --> 01:02:41,130
